course: dictionary
lesson: 7425
title: Polite words in action
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Politely is an adverb. An adverb tells you how something is done. Politely tells you how someone speaks or acts. «Next day he asks politely and plays where they put him.» The word asks tells you the action, and politely tells you the kind, respectful way he asks.
explain: Politely means in a kind, respectful way that shows good manners. Think of a girl whose ball goes over the fence. «She knocks on the door with her dad and asks politely, and the neighbour is happy to hand it back.» Knocking first and asking nicely shows good manners.
explain: Check the meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Try «She asks rudely» and see that it does not show good manners. Then try «She asks politely» and it fits. Politely is the opposite of rudely.
explain: Politely also works when you say no. «It is all right to stop eating when you are full and say politely that you have had enough.» You can refuse kindly, with good manners, and still be honest.
fact: Politely is an adverb, so it tells you how someone does an action.
fact: The forms are politely, more politely and most politely.
fact: «Politely» means in a kind, respectful way that shows good manners.
fact: You can ask politely, answer politely, or say no politely, as in «no, thank you».
fact: Good manners help everyone get along, from shop staff to new people.
exercise: «Next day he asks politely and plays where they put him.» What does politely tell you? | He shouts the question loudly | *He asks in a kind, respectful way | He asks while running away | He never asks at all
exercise: The girl knocks on the neighbour's door and asks politely. What does politely mean here? | In a hurry, without looking up | In a quiet, secret whisper | In a loud, angry way | *In a kind, respectful way
exercise: Which way of saying no is polite? | Whatever, I don't care | Stop being silly, I don't want that | *No, thank you, I have had enough | Go away and leave me alone
test: Which word means nearly the same as politely? | *Respectfully | Loudly | Rudely | Quickly
test: Mia has to leave a party early. Which is the most polite way to say it? | I'm off, bye. | *Thank you, I have had a lovely time, but I must go now. | I'm leaving now and you can't stop me. | Leave me alone, I'm going.
exam: Lisa wants the last biscuit. Which sentence asks politely? | Mine. Don't touch it. | Whoever wants it can grab it. | Give me that biscuit now! | *May I have the last biscuit, please?
